# Shared edge proxy for the WHOLE VPS — the only binder of ports 80/443, shared by every
# project on the box (cosimosi, postpilot, whatever comes third). Lives at /srv/edge,
# managed by hand: edit → validate → `docker compose up -d`. It is NOT part of any
# project's deploy workflow; api stacks roll independently underneath it.
#
# This file is a router, not a config. Every site block lives in its own
# conf.d/<project>.caddy fragment, so adding a project is "drop one file, append one .env
# line" instead of a merge conflict in a file everyone shares.
#
# Ownership contract (DEPLOY.md §4):
#   Caddyfile, docker-compose.yml  → the BOX, not a project. They stay as they are however
#                                    many projects land; only global options belong here.
#   conf.d/<project>*.caddy        → that project's repo alone owns its own files.
#   .env                           → append your PREFIXED domain vars; touch no one else's.
#
# Caddy config is all-or-nothing: one broken fragment takes TLS down for EVERY project on
# the box, not just its own. Always validate the assembled config before reloading:
#
#   docker run --rm -v /srv/edge/Caddyfile:/etc/caddy/Caddyfile:ro \
#     -v /srv/edge/conf.d:/etc/caddy/conf.d:ro --env-file /srv/edge/.env \
#     caddy:2-alpine caddy validate --config /etc/caddy/Caddyfile --adapter caddyfile
#
# Global options (ACME email, etc.) go here and must be the FIRST block, before the import
# — that is the one reason to edit this file:
# {
# 	email ops@example.com
# }

# Relative to this file, i.e. /etc/caddy/conf.d/ in the container. An empty conf.d is safe:
# a glob matching nothing warns and passes. Order is irrelevant — distinct site addresses
# are order-independent.
import conf.d/*.caddy
